// EXPECT: block comments are not supported
// The same mistake as block_comment.id, but at the top of the file rather
// than inside a function -- which is where a header comment actually goes,
// and so the case a newcomer hits first.
//
// Only the statement parser asked "is this token an operator?", so at top
// level `/*` was taken as a function *name*, the emitter wrote
// `int id_/*(int int, int ,, IdList* argv);`, and `cc` rejected it -- which
// bin/idc reported as "this is a bug in the self-hosted compiler; please
// report it", inviting a bug report about the user's own typo. idc.py had
// always got this right, so it was also a diagnostic-parity hole that
// tests/invalid.sh could not see, because its only case was in a body.
/* a header comment, written the way every other language spells it */
main(int argc, string[] argv) {
  print(1);
} return int 0;
